{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` sits at **798 lines against the locked 800-LOC hard cap** — two lines of headroom. The next change that adds three lines to it fails CI, and there is no allowlist and no way to raise the limit (`.github/module-size-limit.txt` is locked; a contributor change to it fails the build by design).\n\nThis is not hypothetical. Change 2359 (`bd66-28a4-fd31-4c9f`) already hit it: the file was 788 on main, 2359 added 45, and CI refused at 833 on all four platforms. That change landed by relocating `run_auto_maintenance` into `event_commit_git.py` and compressing a comment block — which cleared the cap but left the file at 798, so the problem is deferred rather than solved. Several other in-flight changes are growing `src/rebar` modules concurrently.\n\n### What the size test enforces, and where RED begins\n\n`tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py::test_no_src_module_over_the_hard_cap` mirrors the CI gate and enforces **only the 800-line hard cap** — it does NOT enforce the 200-500 target. So at 798 it is **already GREEN**, and this work does NOT flip it RED to GREEN; it keeps it GREEN with room to spare. Measured on this tree:\n\n| `gitutil.py` lines | contract test |\n|---|---|\n| 798 (today, `origin/main`) | PASSES |\n| 801 (append three blank lines) | FAILS: \"src/rebar file(s) over the 800-LOC hard cap\" |\n| 373 (after this split) | PASSES |\n\nThe RED boundary is therefore exactly **three added lines** away. The proving evidence for this ticket is the measured post-split LOC and the restored headroom, not a RED-to-GREEN transition.\n\n## The seam — it already exists in the file\n\n`gitutil.py`'s own module docstring carves out a self-contained policy essay, \"Blocking policy under local git lock contention\". That is the boundary: **`gitutil` RUNS a git subprocess; the extracted layer DECIDES what happens when git's own locks are contended.** The dependency runs one way (`gitutil` -> `git_locking`).\n\n### Exactly what moves to `git_locking.py`\n\nFunctions: `_store_git_op_lock`, `_store_git_lock_path`, `fetch_coordination_lock`, `_resolve_common_git_dir`, `_acquire_fetch_coord_flock`, `_with_index_lock_retry`, `_reclaim_if_stale_index_lock`, `_augment_lock_exhaustion`, `_is_index_lock_error`, `_is_git_lock_error`, `_jitter`, `_backoff_sleep`, `_lock_backoff_s`, `_lock_retry_budget_s`, `_resolve_tracker_git_dir`.\n\nModule-level state the moved functions read as globals — these MUST travel with their readers or the readers see a stale/absent global: `_INDEX_LOCK_STALE_S`, `_INDEX_LOCK_ATTEMPTS`, `_INDEX_LOCK_BACKOFF_S`, `_INDEX_LOCK_BACKOFF_CAP_S`, `_STORE_GIT_LOCK_NAME`, `_STORE_GIT_LOCK_WAIT_S`, `_STORE_GIT_LOCK_POLL_S`, `_FETCH_COORD_LOCK_NAME`, `_FETCH_COORD_WAIT_S`, `_FETCH_COORD_POLL_S`, `_LOCK_PATH_RE`, `_LOCK_REF_RE`, and the two test probes `_retry_probe` / `_reclaim_probe`.\n\n### What stays in `gitutil.py`\n\n`run_git`, `run_git_bounded`, `run_git_write`, `_with_transient_fault_retry` and its `_TRANSIENT_*` tables, `is_transient_object_read_error`, `_is_transient_object_write_error`, `_is_transient_git_fault`, `_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT`, `_AUTOMAINT_OFF`, `_ticket_dirs`, `_dir_is_archived`, `path_is_foreign_to_branch`, `discard_unmerged_paths`.\n\n### Re-exports `gitutil` must keep (production importers)\n\n`src/rebar/_store/event_append.py:103` imports `gitutil._INDEX_LOCK_STALE_S`; `src/rebar/_store/lock.py:71` imports `gitutil._backoff_sleep, _jitter`; `_commands/fsck_recover.py`, `_commands/fsck_repair.py` and `_commands/bridge_repair.py` import `gitutil._resolve_tracker_git_dir`; `_snapshot/git_fetch.py` and `_store/sync.py` import `gitutil.fetch_coordination_lock`. These are covered by re-export and require no change.\n\n### Test modules that patch a moved symbol ON the module object (re-export does NOT cover these)\n\n`tests/interfaces/store/test_git_contention_9305.py`, `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_delete_init_index_lock.py`, `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_compact_index_lock.py`, `tests/interfaces/store/test_event_append_index_lock.py`, `tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_index_lock_reclaim_toctou.py`, `tests/interfaces/store/test_stale_lock_reclaim_write.py`, `tests/unit/test_fetch_common_dir_coordination.py`. `tests/unit/store/test_git_outcome.py` patches `\"rebar._store.gitutil._backoff_sleep\"` in string form but its READER (`_with_transient_fault_retry`) stays in `gitutil`, so it must NOT be retargeted.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` passes (it passes before the work too; it must not regress).\n- [ ] `gitutil.py` is within the 200-500 LOC target.\n- [ ] The new `git_locking.py` is within the 200-500 LOC target.\n- [ ] Every moved function body is byte-identical to its original, shown by a `difflib` comparison of the moved line ranges, not by eye.\n- [ ] The retained half of `gitutil.py` differs from its pre-split content only in the docstring and the import block; no executable line changes.\n- [ ] The same test set passes before and after the split, with no test moving from passing to failing.\n- [ ] Every `monkeypatch.setattr` aimed at a symbol whose reader MOVED is retargeted to `git_locking`.\n- [ ] Every `monkeypatch.setattr` aimed at a symbol whose reader STAYED is left on `gitutil`.\n- [ ] Each retarget is shown to be load-bearing by the evidence described under Testing.\n- [ ] `gitutil`'s production importers require no change, shown by importing every `rebar` module without error.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Compare moved line ranges against the new module with `difflib` and show the diff is empty apart from join whitespace — do not eyeball it.\n- **`monkeypatch.setattr` is the dangerous part and needs explicit proof.** A patch aimed at the wrong module is **silently vacuous**, not a loud failure: the test still passes while patching nothing. A green suite is NOT evidence. For each retargeted patch, demonstrate it still takes effect by ONE of:\n  - **Mis-target probe** — aim the patch at the module the reader left, and show the run fails loudly (`monkeypatch.setattr` defaults to `raising=True`, so a name absent from that module raises `AttributeError`).\n  - **Seeded mutant** — for a name that IS still present on both modules (`_backoff_sleep`), seed the behaviour the patch is meant to observe and show the correct target notices it while the old target passes vacuously.\n  - **Invocation probe** — for a patch deliberately LEFT on `gitutil`, record calls and assert the patched callable actually fired.\n- Grep for the module path and every moved symbol **as a STRING** (`monkeypatch.setattr`, `getattr`, `importlib`, patch targets, docs references). An LSP cannot see these, so a semantic search returning nothing is not evidence of absence.\n- Run `make lint` (including the complexity ratchet: `new=0 increased=0`) and `make typecheck` to zero, plus `check_comment_hygiene`, `check_raw_git_writes`, `check_wall_clock_asserts`.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787973934151638001,"uuid":"47253646-6559-4786-aa1e-e24ddba6aa72"}